Original commit body from 7d74b656bf32210d2873a58118758cdfc87d3d21:

Two runs of the identical binary on an unchanged repo reported DIFFERENT paths
for the same flow -- same source, same sink, same label, different middle hop,
both hops genuine callers. Adjacency is set-valued, so BFS dequeue order
followed str set iteration and therefore PYTHONHASHSEED; the parent map
recorded whichever predecessor happened to arrive first. Measured on kserve:
2 of 182 distinct flows differed run to run.

CAUSE CONFIRMED BEFORE FIXING, NOT ASSUMED. A direct probe with eight equally-
valid middle hops: a fixed seed produced an identical path twice, and five
different seeds produced four distinct witnesses (midA / midF / midE / midF /
midC). The item was filed with the cause marked "suspected"; this is what
promoted it.

NOT COSMETIC. A user diffing two runs on an unchanged repo saw evidence churn
with no cause, which trains them to distrust the diff. It also made any
path-level A/B between builds unreliable -- it cost real time during the
WI-kabif measurement, where a first reading of "30 flows appeared and
disappeared" turned out to be entirely this artifact.

FIXED BY A DECLARED TIE-BREAK, AND THE FIELD NOW SAYS WHAT IT IS. BFS visits
each node's callees in sorted id order, so the witness is a deterministic
function of the input. The item explicitly warned against doing only that and
continuing to call the result "the path", so path's docstring now states it is
ONE witness among possibly several equally-valid routes, that the others are
real and unlisted, and that a consumer needing "every route" must not read it
as if it were that.

SCOPE MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. Both propagators shared the defect (they
share _build_adjacency), and after the sanitizer-barrier refactor the entire
blast radius is TWO LINES in one helper. Repo-wide sweep for the same shape
found one other set-iterating traversal (compact.py's component-root
accumulation), which is order-independent -- it accumulates into a set and
reads only len().

VERIFIED AGAINST THE ITEM'S OWN FILED REPRO: kserve, two runs, 0 flows with a
differing path (was 2). Pinned by an in-process test asserting the specific
declared winner (with a non-vacuity floor proving the fixture really offers
competing witnesses) plus a subprocess test across five hash seeds -- the
latter contributes no coverage, and exists because the tie-break assertion is
a proxy while the cross-seed run is the property itself.

